    "problem_state": {
      "type": "problem-statement",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow_text": "Why a spike detox now",
        "heading": "Modern Spike Burden Is Real",
        "paragraph_1": "Cardiovascular health depends on a quiet, ongoing process: the body's natural fibrinolytic system, which keeps fibrin in check so blood flows clean through arteries and capillaries. When that system runs slow, fibrin can persist in the vasculature — and researchers have documented anomalous fibrin-amyloid microclots in post-viral populations that resist normal clearance. The conversation has moved well beyond cholesterol numbers.",
        "paragraph_2": "Most spike-detox supplements on the shelf bury their nattokinase dose inside a proprietary blend, hide the FU activity behind the supplement-facts panel, or sit at 2,000 FU — a regulatory minimum, not a clinically active range. The largest peer-reviewed trial to date (Chen 2022, n=1,062) identified 6,000–12,000 FU/day as the active range.",
        "paragraph_3": "We built Spike Detox to be the opposite: one ingredient, the FU number on the front of the label, and a per-capsule dose you stack to your protocol with your clinician.",
        "layout_image_side": "image_right"
      }
    },
    "origin": {
      "type": "origin-story",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "Origin",
        "heading": "Miyazaki, 1980. The accident that became a category.",
        "paragraph_1": "Dr. Hiroyuki Sumi, a researcher at Miyazaki Medical College, was looking for natural substances that dissolve fibrin. He tested 173 foods on Petri dishes coated with artificial blood clots.",
        "paragraph_2": "Most foods did nothing. One traditional Japanese fermented soybean dish — natto — cleared the clot in under 18 hours. Sumi named the responsible enzyme nattokinase.",
        "paragraph_3": "Forty years later, peer-reviewed cardiovascular research has built on that single moment. Spike Detox is part of that lineage — one ingredient, transparently dosed.",
        "pull_quote": "\"The most fibrinolytically active food I have ever encountered.\"",
        "pull_quote_attribution": "Dr. Hiroyuki Sumi, Miyazaki Medical College",
        "layout_image_side": "image_left"
      }
    },
